hing_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grid contains.** Every non-background thing in these grids is a small solid square, 2×2 or 3×3. Each square is filled with a two-colour checkerboard. No two squares touch, so each one is a single multicolour object.\n\n**What changes.** Comparing input and output, every square keeps its rows and its pattern exactly. Only its x position changes. All squares are pushed to the right, as if gravity pulled toward the right edge. A square stops either at the grid border or against a square that has already stopped and shares at least one of its rows.\n\n- **Pair 1.**\n  - The block at rows 3–4 (x7–8) slides to x13–14.\n  - The block at rows 2–3 shares row 3 with it, so it stops just left of it, at x11–12.\n  - The block at rows 7–8 goes to x13–14. The block at rows 8–9 stops against it at x11–12.\n  - The block at rows 10–11 has nothing in its way and goes to x13–14.\n- **Pair 2.**\n  - The 3×3 at rows 7–9 reaches x12–14.\n  - The squares at rows 5–7 and 9–11 each share a row with it, so both stop at x9–11.\n  - The square at rows 1–3 is unobstructed and reaches x12–14.\n- **Pair 3.** The same stacking happens. For example, the block at rows 1–2 stops against the 3×3 at rows 2–4.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Find the squares and process them from the rightmost one leftward. Each square is slid right until it reaches the border or a cell already occupied in any of its rows.\n2. Start from an empty canvas. Placing each square is one meaningful edit, so I emit the canvas after each placement.\n\n**Test input.**\n- The 3×5 block at rows 2–6 slides to x10–14.\n- The 3×3 block at rows 10–12 also goes to x12–14, since nothing blocks those rows.\n- The 3×3 block at rows 0–2 shares row 2 with the big block, so it stops at x7–9.\n- The 2×2 block at rows 5–6 stops against the big block at x8–9.\n- The 2×2 blocks at rows 9–10 and 13–14 both stop against the 3×3 at rows 10–12, landing at x10–11.\n- Rows 13–14 contain nothing in the way except that 3×3's row 12, which doesn't overlap rows 13–14. So the block at rows 13–14 instead runs to the border at x13–14.\n\nThe code handles all of this by checking actual occupied cells row by row.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, multicolour=True)\n    # rightmost objects settle first\n    objs.sort(key=lambda o: -o[\"box\"][3])\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    W = g.shape[1]\n    for o in objs:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        cells = o[\"cells\"]\n        shift = 0\n        while True:\n            ns = shift + 1\n            ok = True\n            for (y, x) in cells:\n                nx = x + ns\n                if nx >= W or out[y, nx] != bg:\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n            if not ok:\n                break\n            shift = ns\n        for (y, x) in cells:\n            out[y, x + shift] = g[y, x]\n        emit(out, f\"Slide block at rows {y0}-{y1}, cols {x0}-{x1} right by {shift} until it hits the border or a settled block.\")\n    emit(out, \"All blocks have slid right and stacked.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The block at rows 3–4 (x7–8) slides to x13–14."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The 3×5 block at rows 2–6 slides to x10–14."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Slide block at rows 10-11, cols 9-10 right by 4 until it hits the border or a settled block."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"All squares are pushed to the right, as if gravity pulled toward the right edge."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Rows 13–14 contain nothing in the way except that 3×3's row 12, which doesn't overlap rows 13–14."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"f3a1e31734402294c7f72514a4b27fa9ccdaaf5003dec2b1caae14